  "response": "Adjudicating between infection by a soil-borne pathogen and root-zone waterlogging in an oak stand with progressive crown dieback turns on seeking evidence that is diagnostic of one process and not shared with the other, since thinning crowns, tufted foliage and branch mortality attend both. Clarity about the suspected agent comes first. Phytophthora cinnamomi is an oomycete rather than a true fungus. It is classified in the phylum Ascomycota, although being an oomycete rules out placement in that phylum.\n\nPhytophthora infection of oaks produces dark bleeding cankers at the root collar and lower stem. Because such lesions frequently lie out of sight, excavation of the root collar and structural roots is performed to expose lesions hidden below the soil line. After the woody tissue has been uncovered in this way, diagnostic bark samples are taken at the margin between necrotic and apparently healthy inner bark. Baiting rhizosphere soil samples with young oak or rhododendron leaves is a standard method for recovering Phytophthora from the root zone, and the floated baits are incubated for several days before examination. Lateral flow immunoassay devices identify Phytophthora isolates to species level in the field.\n\nWhereas a species name can be put to an isolate on site with a hand-held immunoassay strip, species-level confirmation of the pathogen is obtained by PCR amplification and sequencing of the ITS region from root and soil samples. Once that sequence identity has been established, pathogenicity of the recovered isolate is subsequently tested by inoculating healthy oak seedlings under controlled conditions, which distinguishes an incidental resident of the rhizosphere from the agent responsible for the decline.\n\nWaterlogging kills fine roots by depriving them of oxygen, and a root zone from which oxygen has been excluded in this way cannot be one in which waterlogging raises the soil redox potential. Soil redox potential falls as a soil remains saturated, and this progressive reduction leaves grey gleyed horizons and orange mottling in the soil profile wherever saturation has been prolonged. A soil that grows steadily more reducing the longer it stands saturated cannot at the same time be a soil in which waterlogging has raised the redox potential, and that second account of the root zone must therefore be set aside. Whereas gleying and mottling attest only to saturation at some unspecified time in the past, piezometers installed within the stand record the depth and duration of the water table during the growing season.\n\nThe two lines of evidence are then weighed against each other. A verdict of infection rests on cankers whose margins advance into living bark, on repeated recovery of the organism from the rhizosphere and from lesion edges, and on reproduction of the syndrome in inoculated seedlings; a verdict of asphyxiation rests on a water table standing within the rooting zone for weeks during flush and leaf expansion, on fine-root death confined to the saturated horizon, and on a dieback pattern that follows microtopography rather than the expanding front expected of a spreading pathogen. Because saturated soils both kill absorbing roots and favour the production and dispersal of motile zoospores, the competing explanations often prove to be concurrent, and the diagnostic report should state the share each is judged to carry before silvicultural or drainage measures are prescribed.",
